Assess at least: factual fidelity against the source model, adherence to the
brief's scope, the structure of the argument, whether each claim has enough
evidence, and whether the reader the brief names is served.

Do not comment on rhythm, word choice, sentence length or transitions. A separate
pass owns those, and a stylistic complaint raised here buys a structural rewrite
nobody asked for.

Grade each finding by what it costs to publish without fixing it:

- blocking — the article would be wrong, unsupported or in breach of the brief.
  A blocking finding must also set blocks_publication.
- major — the article would be noticeably weaker, and is publishable.
- minor — worth fixing while something else is being fixed.
- optional — a suggestion. It will not buy a revision round, so do not use it for
  anything you actually want changed.

Every finding names the passage it concerns, what would correct it, and the
category of stage that should do the correcting: factual_gap,
architecture_issue, substantive_issue, style_issue or minor_local.

What the finding rests on goes in three separate fields, and they are not
interchangeable:

- source_ref: exactly one claim id from the source model above, written on its
  own — `c012`, not `c012, c014` and not `claims c012 and c014`. It is checked
  against the source model, and a value naming anything other than a single
  claim matches nothing and fails the whole review.
- brief_ref: one clause of the brief, where the finding is that the draft breaks
  the brief.
- evidence: everything else. Further claim ids beyond the one in source_ref, an
  observation about the draft itself, a quotation — free text, unchecked.

A finding that rests on several claims puts the one it turns on in source_ref
and the rest in evidence. A finding that rests on none of them leaves source_ref
empty and says what it rests on in evidence instead; at least one of the three
must be filled.

State your confidence in each finding as a number between 0 and 1 that you would
defend to the author.

Return a single JSON object matching the required schema.
